Also who fills the comparison? The writer has local facts but may not know run counts. Sun Tzu #11: foreknowledge must come from people who know the enemy's situation, not from spirits or measurement. So: when the writer doesn't know the run count, ask the person who runs it (the owner or the operator); that's paying for foreknowledge. #17: probe/read the signs — try the script first (as W3.6 already says), which gives a measured build cost estimate. The writer tries the script first (W3.6's existing rule) — the attempt gives the build cost and the check cost; then compares with what it saves. This is important: W3.6 already says "The agent writing the procedure decides by trying the script first." So the writer already attempts the script; the new sentence should turn "trying" into "reckoning": try enough to price the build and the check, then compare.

At what moment: before committing the procedure page, at writing or first revision; the estimate written into the page (or the task's state.md — where should it be written? The question's answer should say where). W3.6's fails_if is about "a procedure page names no step a script cannot do, with no written reason." If we add worth-it, the fails_if needs a "written reason" for the page: the comparison and its numbers. Where written? The existing levels fact says a SHOULD skip reason goes in the task's `state.md`. But a docs page persists beyond the task. Hmm. W3.6 says "with no written reason" in fails_if; presumably reason goes on the page. For a procedure page that stays a page for "not worth it", the reason should live with the page so the next reader can re-open the decision. I'd propose: a short "Why this is a page" note on the page itself (one line naming the comparison and its basis), because the page is the artifact that persists; state.md is per-task and dies. That's a concrete recommendation. But careful: the "one thing" rule and the owner's instruction "never add structure not given" — I'm not adding structure to W3.6 itself, just saying where the reason goes. The question says "what should W3.6 say, in one or two sentences added to its text." I can propose wording that includes "with the comparison and its basis written on the page" (or "recorded on the page"). Keep it one or two sentences.

Test design: a comparison an agent can make, no threshold. The comparison:

For procedure P, estimate over the same horizon (the time the procedure is expected to be in use, or the next N runs if known):
- **Cost of the script:** build cost (to write it and its external check, including the first working check) + keep cost (each scheduled exercise/CI run, each repair when the world moves, review, and the expected cost of a failure it causes, weighted by how likely the exercise misses it) + the cost of the human gate time that remains (a script that stops still spends the person's time).
- **What it saves:** runs × (time per run as a page − time per run as a script) + errors the scripting removes (each error's cost × its frequency as a page) − errors the scripting adds (loud, wide failures, weighted by frequency, and the cost of the check being wrong).
- **Cost of the page:** its upkeep (review/rehearsal) + error cost; the sources say both decay, so include the page's cost of staying current.

Decision: script when the script's saving over the horizon is clearly greater than the script's build+keep cost minus the page's own keep cost; page otherwise. "Clearly" needs a stated margin; but no invented number. How to state it without a number? "greater than" plus "the estimate's error must not swallow the difference" — i.e., the comparison must survive the uncertainty: use the high end of the script's cost and the low end of its saving; if the difference changes sign under those bounds, it's a close case → the page (default) or the owner. That's a comparison an agent can make without a threshold. Also SHOULD's default is script (W3.6's current text says "A procedure lives in the repo as a script"), and the level SHOULD allows skip with reason. Hmm — the default in W3.6 is script ("A procedure lives in the repo as a script with an external check"). But the question is whether it may stay a page. Under a SHOULD, either form is a default with reason? Currently text says script; fails_if fails a page naming no unscriptable step. So currently a "worth it" page fails. The new wording should make the page acceptable when the comparison favors it, and shift the default? I think keep the default "script first" — try the script first — but allow a page when the reckoning says the script costs more than it saves. The prior Sun Tzu answer had: 1,2,3 yes → script; 3 no → page. Now the question is specifically about #3 where 3 is yes ("it will run again on ground that stays still") but rarely, so the old test says script. New: even when it runs again, if the runs are few relative to build+keep, the page is allowed. So the answer is: yes, with a comparison. This refines the prior "3 no → page" into "3 yes but the comparison favors the page".

Actually, importantly, the prior answer's #3 condition already was "will it run again, close enough to the same ground? Count runs against build plus upkeep." It said "3 no → page". So the prior answer already had a comparison in #3; it just said count runs against build+upkeep but didn't specify the comparison. This new question asks to make it checkable and decide who. So I can build on the prior answer honestly: the prior test had the comparison's terms but not its form; this answer supplies the form and the decider. That's a nice continuity and shows I re-read.

One subtlety: "because it runs rarely" alone is not a sufficient reason. The source A4 (Google SRE) says work that does not repeat is not toil by definition; A2 says keeping a script is ongoing cost; B10 says rarely-run automation is fragile; the xkcd table compares build time against runs × saved time; Gremlin says pages drift. So "rarely" matters only through the comparison and through fragility: rare runs make the keep cost higher and the feedback worse, and reduce the saving. So a rare procedure is a candidate for a page, not automatically a page. A procedure run once a year but whose failure costs a lot (dangerous, irreversible) may still warrant a script because the error reduction is large (like safety-critical maintenance, C20). And a procedure run often but cheaply may also stay a page. So the test is the comparison, not the frequency. That's a key point: frequency is one term in the comparison, never the decision by itself. The brief's fact from Linus says "cost judgment, not technical impossibility". Good.

Also "a page" here must not copy the procedure's steps into memory; it lives in docs/. The worth-it page is still a page with the steps, and it must call scripts for the parts that are scripts. Fine.

What should W3.6 say? One or two sentences added. Proposed wording (my own, not a quotation):

"A procedure that could be a script may stay a page when the agent writing it reckons, over the runs it expects, that building and keeping the script — its external check, its schedule of exercise, its repair as the ground moves, and the failures it would still miss — would cost more than it saves; the reckoning's terms and the facts behind them are written on the page, and a close reckoning goes to the project owner."

Hmm, that's one long sentence. Maybe two:

"A procedure may stay a page, even where every step could be a command, when the agent writing it compares the script's build and keep cost — the external check, a schedule that exercises it, repairs as the ground moves, and the failures it would still miss — with what the script saves over the runs it expects, and the page wins. That comparison and the facts behind it are written on the page, and the project owner decides when the two sides are close, the facts are missing, or the choice is contested."

