Let me draft:

Addition (two sentences):
"A procedure may stay a page when every step could be a command, if the agent writing it compares the script's build and keep cost — its external check, a schedule that exercises it, repairs as the ground moves, and the failures it would still miss — against what the script saves over the runs it expects, and the page is the lesser cost. The comparison and its basis are written on the page; when the two sides are close, or its basis is missing, the project owner decides, not the writer."

Hmm, "and the page is the lesser cost" — but the page itself has a keep cost too. The comparison should be between the two forms' total costs, not "the script's cost vs what it saves" (which ignores the page's cost). The brief asks "for example, the cost of building and keeping the script against what it saves". So "what it saves" is relative to the page. So: compare script's build+keep against what it saves over the page (the page's own keep/error cost being the baseline). That's fine.

But is "the page is the lesser cost" a threshold? No, it's a comparison. Good. But "lesser cost" is a bit loose about risk. Add "clearly" maybe. "and the page costs less once both forms' keep costs are counted." Better.

Also, important: the page must not be chosen merely because it's not built; the page has a keep cost too and a failure cost. The comparison must include the page's expected error cost (out of practice, omitted step, drift). The sources C16–C19 and the HSE omissions. So the test must not compare build cost against zero; it compares two whole forms. If the page's error cost is high (safety-critical), the script may win even if rare. That's the "page is the lesser cost once both forms' keep costs are counted" clause. Good.

The formula I'll give:

For a procedure P, over the horizon H it is expected to be in use (or the next R runs if known), compare:

**Script side:** B (build: steps + external check, first working) + K_s (keep: scheduled exercise, repair, review, deps) + G (the human gate time that remains) + E_s (expected cost of script failures, weighted by how often the exercise would miss them).
**Page side:** K_p (keep: review/rehearsal) + G (the same gate time, if any) + E_p (expected cost of page errors: omission, drift, out-of-practice).

Choose script when (K_p + E_p) − (B + K_s + E_s) is positive and larger than the error in the estimate; choose page otherwise. If the sign is not clear under the high/low ends of the estimate, it's a close call: page (the cheap form, reversible) and the owner decides whether to pay for the script. Hmm — which default for close calls? Options: (a) page, because the owner's rule says SHOULD may be skipped with a reason, and the cheap form is reversible; (b) owner decides. I think: the close case goes to the owner, because it's a value judgment (what the project is willing to pay now for a future saving). But the current default is script. If close → owner, and SHOULD allows the writer to leave the page with a reason until the owner answers, that matches prior "owner decides the open case". I like: clear → writer; close or contested → owner; missing facts → the writer asks the person who runs it, and if that fails, page with the open question recorded (not silent script).

Also, there's an asymmetry worth stating: a script's failure is loud and can be wide (B12), and a rarely-run script can be untested (B10) and can break silently if not exercised (B11, B15). A page's failure is silent drift (C16). The sources say both decay; the difference is visibility. So the keep cost of the script must include the schedule; if no one will exercise it, it's not "kept", and the comparison should price it as unexercised (high E_s) — which often favors the page. Conversely, a script that will be run often enough naturally gets exercised, lowering E_s. That's a nice frequency mechanism: frequency is a proxy for feedback quality, not just saved time. The research says B13 "if automation runs regularly and successfully enough... reduced MTTR" and B14 "frequency reduces difficulty". So the test captures both the saved time and the error rate.

How to decide who at what moment: The writer at the moment of writing (or first revision), before the page is committed; the comparison is recorded on the page (one line). The owner sets the policy once (what counts as "close" — no, that would be a threshold); better: the owner decides the close/contested case when it arises, and the owner may also delegate a standing value (e.g. "we do not build scripts for one-off migrations") — but that's the owner's value, not a number. Don't add structure not given. The question asks who decides: "the agent writing the procedure, the project owner, or someone else — and at what moment." So my answer: the writer decides the clear case, in writing, at the moment the procedure is written or first revised, because they hold the facts and can try the script; the owner decides the close case and any contested one, when it is raised, before the page is accepted. The owner also may decide up front for a class of procedures (e.g. one-off/throwaway) — that's a value, and it saves appeals. Hmm, but "never add structure not given" — proposing the owner set a class-level value is not adding structure to W3.6, it's answering who decides. Could be useful but might be overreach. Keep it conditional: if the owner wants to keep their time, they can say so once for a class; otherwise each close case goes to them.

Wait: W3.6's existing who_decides = "the agent writing the procedure, by trying the script first; the project owner if contested". The new sentence can preserve that, adding "the comparison and its basis written on the page". So who_decides becomes: writer for the clear case, owner for close/contested. That's a small extension of "if contested" — a close comparison is a kind of contest with the facts. The new wording should say it.

Now for the "how I decided" section: numbered steps each citing my #n or "my reading". I should aim ~8–10 steps, like the prior answer. Re-read the list and use relevant items:

1. **#1 Reckon before battle.** The question is a choice between two grounds; lay it out and compare. Quote 「夫未戰而廟算勝者…多算勝，少算不勝，而況於無算乎？」. The "five matters / seven comparisons" gives the shape: compare on named terms, not a feeling. And the chain 「地生度，度生量，量生數，數生稱，稱生勝」 — ground first, then quantity/number, then weighing. So: first the ground (who runs, how often, what it touches), then the costs, then the weighing. Choosing "page because rare" without the count is 無算. Good.

2. **#5 Move only on advantage.** 「非利不動，非得不用，非危不戰。…合於利而動，不合於利而止。」 This is the direct rule for "worth it": the script is a move; if the move does not accord with advantage (the comparison), do not make it. This is the heart: "worth it" is 利, not a separate policy. And the risk side is #6.

3. **#6 Mix harm into benefit.** 「是故智者之慮，必雜於利害…」 and 「故不盡知用兵之害者，則不能盡知用兵之利也。」 So the comparison must price both forms' harm: the script's keep cost and loud failure; the page's drift and omission. Do not compare build cost only. This is the method for the test.

4. **#2 Make yourself unbeatable first.** 「先為不可勝，以待敵之可勝…故曰：勝可知，而不可為。」 The check is the ground; a script without an external check is not a script that can win. W3.6 already requires the external check. The check is also part of the build cost. And "victory cannot be made": you cannot make a rare procedure's script pay off by hoping; the comparison tells you.

5. **#4 Know whether you can fight, and the ground.** 「知可以戰與不可以戰者勝」 and the half-victory line 「…而不知地形之不可以戰，勝之半也」. The "can" test is the prior answer's; the "worth" question is the ground/terrain: whether the ground (frequency, stability, who runs) supports the campaign. That's a good framing: W3.6 now covers 知可以戰 (can), this addition covers 地形 (whether the ground permits the fight). Nice.

6. **#8 Value victory, not duration; speed.** 「故兵聞拙速，未睹巧之久也」 — do not run a long build campaign for a small victory. Building and keeping a script is a campaign; for a rare procedure the campaign outlasts the gain. Also 「故兵貴勝，不貴久」 — value the win, not the duration. So a beautiful script that takes long to build and keep is not a virtue. This supports "the page may win".

7. **#14 Choose by the ratio of forces.** 「十則圍之，五則攻之…不若則能避之。故小敵之堅，大敵之擒也。」 — the ratio decides the action; do not fight a stronger force stubbornly. The "force" here is the cost. A small gain against a large build+keep cost is the small force holding stubbornly. This is the closest to a ratio comparison, and it gives the "close case" advice: when not a match, avoid (page), don't force the script. Note the text says "avoid", not "wait for the owner"; but the owner is the one who can raise the force (pay for the build). Good: "not a match, avoid" → page; "equal, you can fight" → the writer may script if they choose; "double, divide" etc. Nice analogy, label as analogy.

8. **#13 Not every road is taken.** 「途有所不由…君命有所不受。」 — a script is a road; not every road is taken. The writer on the ground may decline to take the scripting road when the ground says no; that is the discretion W3.6 already gives by "trying the script first". But the discretion needs a sign, not a mood: the comparison is the sign.

9. **#16 Orders clear first, then enforced.** 「約束不明，申令不熟，將之罪也…」 — W3.6's rule must name the comparison and where it is written; an unclear "worth it" is the rule-writer's fault, and the agent will otherwise invent a reason (or script everything). Also #16's "care that cannot command is useless" maybe not. The owner's rule on thresholds: never invent a number; the comparison is the order.

10. **#11 Foreknowledge from people, pay for it.** 「先知者，不可取於鬼神，不可象於事，不可驗於度，必取於人，知敵之情者也。」 — the run count and the pain of the page are known by the person who runs it; ask them, do not infer from a guess or a number. This decides where missing facts go (and who pays: the owner/operator's time). Also #17 probe: try the script first — that is the cheapest way to get the build cost (W3.6 already says this). Quote 「故策之而知得失之計，作之而知動靜之理…」 — probe to learn.

11. **#19 Name the ground, act as prescribed.** The nine grounds table is the shape of the test: name the condition, then the response. Quote the first lines 「是故散地則無戰，輕地則無止，爭地則無攻…」. This supports a table from condition to response rather than a blanket rule. But careful: the test is a comparison, not a terrain table; use it only for form (a decision rule with no fixed form, #10). Maybe combine with #10 "no constant form": 「故兵無成勢，無恒形，能因敵變化而取勝者，謂之神。」 — neither "always script" nor "always page"; decide by the ground. That's a strong step.

