# 孫子 (Sun Tzu) — When a scriptable procedure may stay a page

## Answer

Yes: a procedure that passes the "can" test may stay a page when the agent writing it reckons, over the runs it expects, that building and keeping the script would cost more than the script saves — measured against the page's own keep and error cost, not against zero, and with the reckoning written down. The writer makes that reckoning at the moment of writing (or first revision) and decides the clear case; the project owner decides when the two sides are close, the facts behind them are missing, or the choice is contested — and the owner may set that value once for a class of procedures (for example, throwaway tasks) so it is not re-litigated.

**The comparison an agent makes.** For procedure P, over the horizon H it is expected to be in use (or the next R runs if R is known), in one unit (time or money):

- **Script cost** = build (steps + an external check that fails on a deliberately broken run, and the first working run) + keep (the scheduled exercise or CI run, repairs as the ground moves, review) + the failures the exercise would still miss (their cost × how often they slip through) + any gate time the script still spends.
- **Page cost** = keep (review, rehearsal) + its errors (omitted step, drift, the follower out of practice; cost × frequency) + the same gate time if a person must still act.
- **Choose the script when page cost − script cost is positive at both ends of the estimate** (the high end of the script's cost against the low end of its saving); if the sign changes between the ends, the case is close. **"Rare" enters only through these terms** — fewer runs lower the saving and weaken the feedback a script needs — it is never the decision by itself. A page is not free, and a script is not free; the comparison is between two forms, not between the script and nothing.

