# 孫子 (Sun Tzu) — A scriptable procedure that runs rarely

## Answer

Yes, it may stay a page — but **not because it runs rarely**. Rarity is one term in a comparison, never the decision: a scriptable procedure may stay a page when the agent writing it reckons that the script's whole cost (build + keep + the failures its check still misses) is greater than the page's whole cost (its own keep + its errors + the time it still takes per run) over the runs the procedure is expected to have, and writes that reckoning on the page. The writer makes the reckoning at the moment of writing (or first revision, for a page that predates the rule) and decides the clear case; the project owner decides when the comparison is close, when its facts are missing, or when the choice is contested, and the owner's value is set once, in the rule itself, not re-litigated per procedure.

Wait, "the owner's value is set once, in the rule itself" — but the wording is being decided now. Fine.

Then the test:

**The comparison.** For procedure P, over the horizon H it is expected to be in use (or the next R runs, if R is known), in one unit (time or money), compare the two whole forms:

| | Script | Page |
|---|---|---|
| one-time | build: the steps + an external check that fails on a deliberately broken run + the first working run | — |
| keep, over H | scheduled exercise / CI, repairs as the ground moves, review | review / rehearsal |
| each run | the part the script performs + any gate a person still holds | the whole procedure by hand + any gate |
| errors | failures the exercise would miss (cost × frequency) | omitted steps, drift, an out-of-practice follower (cost × frequency) |

Choose the form with the lower total over H. State each term as a range with its basis (who was asked, what was tried); if the sign of the difference is the same at both ends, the case is clear; if it changes, the case is close. The same comparison can be read as a computed break-even run count — an output of the terms, never an input threshold. The page option includes any scripts it calls, so the comparison is between the whole forms, not between a script and a bare page.

Then the W3.6 wording:

**What W3.6 should add (two sentences, my wording).** *"A procedure that could be a script may stay a page when the agent writing it compares the script's build and keep cost — its external check, a schedule that exercises it, repairs as the ground moves, and the failures it would still miss — with the page's own keep and error cost over the runs it expects, and the page costs less. The comparison and the facts behind it are written on the page; where it is close or its facts are missing, the project owner decides."*

Then the note:

For the record: `who_decides` should read "the agent writing the procedure, by trying the script first and writing the comparison; the project owner when the comparison is close, its facts are missing, or the choice is contested", and `fails_if` should fail a cost-chosen page that names no comparison (a page kept for an unscriptable step still names that step).

